c1_night.text:
On the second night out of Tollmere, the first fire-arrow arrives. It hits a wain loaded with straw and tinder, and the wain becomes a torch.

*Wood burns,* Aymer said. The riders in grey have come, and they are shooting at the wagons, not at the people.

{{skull_kept|The Skull's box is in Anselm's wagon. It is the third from the front.}}{{skull_in_hills|The Skull is far from here, but the lich's riders do not know that. They are burning wagons to find it.}}

c5_betrayal.text:
It happens at midnight. You are on the bridge with Roland when the postern in the mill-house wall swings inward, and a man you trusted steps out into the torchlight.

{{alcuin_spy|It is Alcuin. His face is wet. "I'm sorry," he says. "I'm so sorry. He has my sister."}}{{!alcuin_spy|It is Pell, the clerk, hollow-eyed. He drew the bolts himself.}}

Behind him, in the dark of the lock-house, something grey is already moving.

c5_champion.text:
A figure steps onto the far end of the bridge: armoured, tall, bareheaded, with a face you know.

{{knows_varric|It is Lord Varric of the Western March, whom the Ford made undead. The lich has raised him to hold the bridge.}}{{!knows_varric|He wears the colours of a lord of your own kingdom. His eyes are milk.}}

He raises a sword, and waits.

c7_l3.text:
Rows of stone bunks. A long, low hall. In every bunk, a body: wet, grey, motionless. Six hundred of them, or more. They sleep with their eyes open.

At the far end of the hall, a man in rusted mail is chained to a pillar. He is alive. He lifts his head as you approach.

{{bors_taken|It is Sir Bors.}}{{!bors_taken|He is a stranger — a prisoner of the lich, human, half-starved.}}

c7_camp1_roland.text:
Sir Roland has carried the standard since the defile. He sits with his back to a wall, sword across his knees.

"You never asked why I took the standard," he says. "Nobody asked me to. I saw it fall. I couldn't bear to watch it lie there."

{{bond_roland|You hold his shoulder. It's the closest thing to a prayer either of you has.}}

c7_camp1_durgan.text:
Durgan sharpens his hammer by the fire, stone on iron, a hypnotic sound.

"Three hundred years I lived here," he says, "and I never once walked past the Hoard. I heard it. I always heard it. Now I will see what it is."

{{durgan_crown|He touches the crown at his belt and says nothing.}}

